1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,503
Functional fMRI data has high temporal
resolution but low spatial resolution and

2
00:00:05,503 --> 00:00:06,879
significant geometric distortion.

3
00:00:06,879 --> 00:00:11,924
To localize activations onto gyral
anatomy, we must accurately align the

4
00:00:11,924 --> 00:00:16,510
functional images to the participant's own
high-resolution T1 structural scan.

5
00:00:22,033 --> 00:00:25,974
Rather than matching raw voxel
intensities, FSL uses Boundary-Based

6
00:00:25,974 --> 00:00:27,288
Registration, or BBR.

7
00:00:27,288 --> 00:00:33,419
BBR segments the white matter boundary on
the T1 scan and evaluates the intensity

8
00:00:33,419 --> 00:00:36,922
gradient across that boundary in the
functional image.

9
00:00:36,922 --> 00:00:40,863
This makes it remarkably resilient to EPI
susceptibility distortions.

10
00:00:45,833 --> 00:00:49,988
In the FEAT registration report, examine
the red outline representing the

11
00:00:49,988 --> 00:00:53,388
structural white matter boundary overlaid
on the functional image.

12
00:00:53,388 --> 00:00:57,920
Look closely at the lateral ventricles,
the corpus callosum, and the Sylvian

13
00:00:57,920 --> 00:00:58,298
fissure.

14
00:00:58,298 --> 00:01:02,830
If the red lines sit outside cortical
boundaries, registration has failed and

15
00:01:02,830 --> 00:01:03,963
must be rerun.

16
00:01:09,567 --> 00:01:13,636
Within-subject functional-to-structural
alignment must always use 6 degrees of

17
00:01:13,636 --> 00:01:14,088
freedom.

18
00:01:14,088 --> 00:01:19,966
Choose BBR as your cost function, and
verify the red outline across multiple

19
00:01:19,966 --> 00:01:22,227
slices before accepting statistical
results.
